><td>&#x02212;</td><td/><td>&#x02212;</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Lipid mix, pH 5</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;*</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;*</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x000b1;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x02212;</td></tr><tr><td align="left">pH, Lipid mix</td><td/><td>5.2</td><td/><td>5.2</td><td/><td>5.2</td><td/><td>5.4</td><td/><td>ND</td><td/><td>ND</td><td/><td>ND</td><td/><td align="left">5.2</td><td/><td align="left">5.2</td><td/><td align="left">5.2</td><td/><td>NA</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Content mix, pH 5</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;*</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;*</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x000b1;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x0002b;</td><td/><td>&#x02212;</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>ND indicates not done. NA indicates not applicable (no fusion detected at any pH). In  the seventh row, data are for both lipid and content mixing. Asterisks in the eighth and  tenth rows indicate a decrease in fusion that correlated with a reduced level of cleaved  HA at the cell surface. In the eighth and tenth rows, &#x000b1; indicates decreased fusion activity for V55P after normalization for cell surface expression (see text for details).  &#x000a0;</p></fn><fn id="TFIII-152"><label>&#x02021;</label><p>These values are upper limits. The pH values for 50% proteinase K sensitivity or reactivity with C-HA1 antibody will be between pH 5.6 and 6.2 (see Figs. <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F4">4</xref> and <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">5</xref>).&#x02009;                      &#x000a0;</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><ack><p>We thank Dr. John Skehel for the gift of the site A monoclonal antibody,  Dr. Peter Kim for the <italic>E. coli</italic> expression vector encoding HA2 residues 33  to 127, and Dr. Lukas Tamm for helpful comments on the manuscript.</p></ack><glossary><title>Abbreviations used in this paper</title><def-list><def-item><term>BHA</term><def><p>bromelain-released hemagglutinin</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>dMM</term><def><p>deoxymannojirimycin</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>HA</term><def><p>hemagglutinin</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>RBC</term><def><p>red blood  cell</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>RT</term><def><p>room temperature</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>wt</term><def><p>wild-type</p></def></def-item></def-list></glossary><ref-list><title>References</title><ref id="B1"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Baker</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Agard</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Influenza hemagglutinin: kinetic control of protein function</article-title><source>Structure</source><year>1994</year><volume>2</volume><fpage>907</fpage><lpage>910</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7866741</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B2"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Blacklow</surname><given-names>SC</given-names></name><name><surname>Lu</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>PS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A trimeric subdomain of the simian immunodeficiency virus envelope glycoprotein</article-title><source>Biochemistry</source><year>1995</year><volume>34</volume><fpage>14955</fpage><lpage>14962</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7578108</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B3"><citation citation-type="other">Bodian, D.L. 1992. Structure-based design of an inhibitor of the conformational  change of influenza hemagglutinin. Ph.D. thesis. University of California,  San Francisco, CA. 282 pp.</citation></ref><ref id="B4"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>B